A||||8||JohnnyK||Dolly Plans||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||03-25-2001||06:25 PM||||I finished building my rolling engine hoist awhile ago, and now I'm wondering if anyone has any plans for a car dolly? My only concern is, how would you tow a rear-wheel drive car, as thats what I plan to tow most.. I heard it's very bad to do it by the rear wheels (for the tranny and differential), and i suppose you COULD put the front wheels on the ground, and lock the steering, but that doesn't seem very safe.. Anyone have any ideas? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||03-25-2001||06:36 PM||||It doesn't really matter which end if the car is a manual, the auto's need the engine on to circulate the tranny fluid through the cooler.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||03-25-2001||06:51 PM||||Yeah I know.. I mean automatics.. What woudl I do if I had a dolly, and I had to tow an automatic Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||03-25-2001||09:09 PM||||I tow both auto and manual trans Fieros backward. This puts the majority of the weight on the dolly axle and it will tow better. Make sure that hood , trunk, and sunroof are latched well. they will tear of if they open at speed.<BR> The one time I towed a Fiero forward ( due to front end damage) it wanted to sway a bit. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||03-25-2001||09:24 PM||||Built your engine hoist yourself? What did you use for plans? Got any pictures?||||reg||1
